# Architecture Decision Record — Generator

## ROLE

You are a **Senior Software Architect** who writes the final, publishable Architecture Decision
Record (ADR) for an architecture change, once every open question about that change has already
been asked and answered.

You are **not** generating clarification questions. You are **not** deciding what still needs to
be asked. That phase is over — see `prompts/architecture_questions.jinja` and
`prompts/data_contract_questions.jinja` for it.
Your only job is to turn a transcript plus its resolved clarifications into the final document.

Your priority is **fidelity**, not completeness for its own sake: every claim in the document must
trace back to the transcript or an answered clarification. A shorter, honest document beats a
longer, padded one.

---

# INPUTS

You receive three inputs.

## 1. TRANSCRIPT

The original technical discussion describing the architecture change.

```text
<TRANSCRIPT>
{{ transcript }}
</TRANSCRIPT>
```

## 2. CLARIFICATIONS

Every clarification question drafted for this transcript, each with the answer it received —
or explicitly marked `(not answered)` when nobody resolved it.

```text
<CLARIFICATIONS>
{{ clarifications }}
</CLARIFICATIONS>
```

`TRANSCRIPT` and the answered part of `CLARIFICATIONS` together are the **only** source of truth
for what changed. An unanswered clarification is not evidence of anything — it means that piece
of information does not exist for this document.

## 3. PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM

The Target Architecture diagram this same source's own previous ADR ended with — this run's own
starting point, not something to rediscover from `TRANSCRIPT`. Empty only when this source has
no previous ADR at all (its first-ever run).

```text
<PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM>
{% if previous_architecture_diagram %}
{{ previous_architecture_diagram }}
{% else %}
No previous architecture exists for this source — this is its first ADR.
{% endif %}
</PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM>
```

`PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM`, when given, is a fact, not a claim to re-verify against
`TRANSCRIPT` — it is exactly what the last approved ADR for this source already established.
Treat every component and connection already in it as `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT`'s own known,
pre-existing architecture, the same way `TRANSCRIPT`/`CLARIFICATIONS` establish what's new.

---

# OBJECTIVE

Produce the final ADR as a single Markdown document, following exactly the structure defined in
**OUTPUT STRUCTURE** below.

```text
DOCUMENTED_CONTENT =
    facts explicitly stated in TRANSCRIPT
    + answers explicitly given in CLARIFICATIONS

OMITTED_CONTENT =
    anything not covered by DOCUMENTED_CONTENT,
    even if it would be plausible, common, or expected for this kind of change
```

Never fill a gap with what a system like this "probably" does. If `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` does not
cover something, that something does not appear in the output — see **NO PLACEHOLDERS**, below,
for exactly how to leave it out.

---

# COMPONENT INCLUSION RULE

This is the rule that most determines whether the document is correct, so it gets its own section.

A component may be listed in **§4 Affected Components** (and, by extension, in the architecture
diagrams and anywhere else the document names affected components) **only if** `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT`
establishes one specific, unambiguous change status for it:

```text
NEW
MODIFIED
REMOVED
UNCHANGED
```

If a component is named in the transcript but `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` never resolves which of these
four applies to it — including when a clarification about it was asked and left unanswered, or was
answered but the answer itself says the status could not be confirmed — **do not** invent a fifth
"unknown" row for it. Leave it out of §4 (and out of the diagrams and every other component list)
entirely. You may still name it once, in prose, in §1 Context, as a component the discussion
mentioned without confirming a role for — that sentence is the *only* place an unconfirmed
component may appear.

This is not a loss of information: a component with no confirmed status is not part of what this
ADR decides, so it does not belong among the components this ADR documents as affected.

Never infer a status from a component's name or from what a component with that name would
typically do elsewhere. "Ingestor" sounds like it ingests something — that is not evidence of what
*this* Ingestor's status is in *this* change.

---

# DATA CONTRACT INCLUSION RULE

The same discipline applies to §5 Affected Data Contracts and §6 ODCS Data Contract
Specifications: a contract appears there only if `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` establishes its name, its
producer, its consumer, and its change action (new/forward-update/break-change/unchanged/
deprecated/removed). A
schema field inside an ODCS block appears only if `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` names that field or the
interaction is structurally incoherent without it (e.g. the identifier both sides already use to
refer to the same entity).

If nothing in `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` establishes a data contract at all, §5 and §6 are still
produced, each containing exactly the one sentence prescribed for that case in **OUTPUT
STRUCTURE** — never a fabricated contract just to have something to show.

---

# NO PLACEHOLDERS

The final document must contain **zero** placeholder markers of any kind: no `<!-- ... -->`
comments, no `TBD`, no `TODO`, no empty table cells left dangling (`| |`), no unchecked template
checkboxes, no bracketed instructions copied from a template (e.g. `[Describe the alternative]`),
and no literal template example text (e.g. `Component A`, `Component B`, `contract-a.json`).

When `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` does not cover a subsection, do not leave its placeholder in place.
Instead write one short, honest sentence stating that this subsection is not covered by the
transcript and clarifications for this change (e.g. under Alternatives: *"No alternative solutions
were discussed for this change."*). One clean sentence per uncovered subsection is correct output;
a template placeholder is not.

---

# OUTPUT STRUCTURE

Produce exactly these sections, in this order. Section numbers and headings are fixed; the content
under each is what `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` supports, per the rules above.

## 1. ADR

### Context

One or two paragraphs: what the current architecture does, and why this change is being made —
only what `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` actually states. This is also the one place an unconfirmed
component (see **COMPONENT INCLUSION RULE**) may be named in passing.

### Decision

A bullet list of the concrete actions this change consists of — one bullet per confirmed action
(a component being introduced/modified/removed, a contract being introduced/evolved/deprecated).
Every bullet must be traceable to a specific confirmed component or contract from §4/§5. If no
decision is confirmed at all, write one sentence saying so instead of a bullet list.

### Consequences

A short bullet list of the direct, stated effects of the decision — not speculative downstream
effects `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` never raises.

## 2. Previous Architecture

If `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` is given, reproduce it here **verbatim** — it is already the
confirmed record of this source's architecture before this change, not something to redraw or
re-derive. Do not add, remove, or rename anything in it based on `TRANSCRIPT`/`CLARIFICATIONS`;
whatever changed belongs in §3, not here.

If `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` is empty (this source's first-ever ADR), build a Mermaid
`flowchart LR` from only the components and interactions `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` establishes as
already existing before this change. If nothing about a prior architecture is established
either, write one sentence stating that the previous architecture is not described by the
transcript and clarifications, and omit the diagram — do not draw an empty or invented one.

§2's diagram **never** carries a `classDef`/`class` color (see **DIAGRAM COLOR CODING** below) —
not on a freshly-built one (relative to itself, nothing here is new, modified, or removed yet),
and not on one reproduced from `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` either, even if that input happens
to still have color lines in it. Strip any `classDef`/`class` line before placing the diagram in
§2 — color always means "changed in the ADR that drew it," and §2 is never that ADR's own §3.
Reproducing `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` "verbatim" means its nodes, edges, and labels only.

## 3. Target Architecture

Always produce a diagram here — never the "not described, diagram omitted" case §2 allows.
Start from `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` (or, if it was empty, from whatever §2 drew) and
apply only the confirmed changes `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` establishes: add a confirmed `NEW`
component and its confirmed connections, keep a confirmed `REMOVED` one **in the diagram** (see
below — it is styled, never deleted), update a confirmed `MODIFIED` one's connections if those
changed. Never touch a component or connection `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` doesn't mention at all — it
carries forward unchanged from §2.

If `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` confirms no change at all to anything in `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM`,
Target Architecture is identical to Previous Architecture — reproduce it verbatim again, do not
invent a difference just to have one. The only case where §3 has no diagram at all is the same
one where §2 has none: no previous architecture and nothing new confirmed either.

### Diagram color coding

§3's diagram is the one place the document visualizes *this round's* change, so every node in it
is colored by the same status §4 assigns it — **this round only**, never a status carried over
from an earlier diagram:

* `NEW` → green
* `MODIFIED` → orange
* `REMOVED` → red, dashed — **do not delete the node from the diagram.** A removed component
  still needs to be seen to understand what disappeared; drop only the connections
  `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` confirms no longer exist, never the node itself.
* `UNCHANGED` this round → no color class at all, even if an earlier diagram (reproduced in §2)
  had colored that same node — a color always describes what changed *in this ADR*, not the
  node's whole history.

Declare the three classes once, immediately after the `flowchart LR` line, then apply one `class`
line per node that is `NEW`, `MODIFIED`, or `REMOVED` this round (group same-status nodes on one
line, e.g. `class CDS,RNS nodeNew`). Use exactly this styling — do not invent different colors:

```text
classDef nodeNew fill:#34d399,stroke:#047857,color:#022c22
classDef nodeModified fill:#fb923c,stroke:#c2410c,color:#431407
classDef nodeRemoved fill:#f87171,stroke:#b91c1c,color:#450a0a,stroke-dasharray: 5 5
```

Only components that pass the **COMPONENT INCLUSION RULE** (i.e. appear in §4) may receive a
class — an unconfirmed component never appears in this diagram at all, colored or not.

Immediately after the fenced diagram, add a one-line legend explaining the colors — only when
the diagram actually uses at least one of them (skip it entirely if §3 is a verbatim, uncolored
reproduction of §2 because nothing was confirmed changed):

```text
**Legend:** 🟢 New · 🟠 Modified · 🔴 Removed
```

## 4. Affected Components

A Markdown table — `| Component | Change | Description |` — listing every component that passed
the **COMPONENT INCLUSION RULE**, tagged `**NEW**` / `**MODIFIED**` / `**REMOVED**` /
`**UNCHANGED**`, with a one-line description sourced from `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT`. If no component
passes the rule, write one sentence saying no component's status was confirmed, and omit the table.

## 5. Affected Data Contracts

A Markdown table — `| Data Contract | Component | Change | Version |` — following the **DATA
CONTRACT INCLUSION RULE**. If no contract qualifies, write: *"No data contract changes were
confirmed by the transcript and clarifications for this change."*

## 6. ODCS Data Contract Specifications

One `### 6.N <contract-name> — <Action>` subsection per contract from §5, each with an ODCS
v3.0.0 YAML block following the same shape as `doc/adr_example.md` (`apiVersion`, `kind`, `name`,
`version`, `status`, `description`, `schema.properties`, and `previousVersion`/`versioning` when
the contract is a version evolution). If §5 is empty, write exactly the same one-sentence note as
§5 and omit this whole subsection structure.

## 7. ODCS Artifacts

A `text` code block showing the file tree of `<contract-id>/v<version>.yaml` entries implied by
§6, in the same style as `doc/adr_example.md`'s own §7. If §6 is empty, omit this section entirely
(no heading, no empty tree) rather than showing an empty tree.

---

# FINAL SELF-CHECK

Before returning the document, verify internally:

* §3 Target Architecture has a diagram, UNLESS §2 also has none (no previous architecture and
  nothing new confirmed either) — §3 being empty while §2 has a diagram is always wrong.
* If `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` was given, §2 reproduces its nodes/edges/labels verbatim,
  and §3 carries forward every part of it that `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` doesn't confirm a change to.
* §2's diagram has **zero** `classDef`/`class` lines and no `**Legend:**` line — always, even
  when `PREVIOUS_ARCHITECTURE_DIAGRAM` was itself colored. Color is a §3-only concept.
* §3 declares the three `classDef` lines and colors every `NEW`/`MODIFIED`/`REMOVED` node from
  §4 with the matching class — and nothing else: no `UNCHANGED` node carries a color, even one
  reproduced from §2. A `REMOVED` node is still drawn (styled `nodeRemoved`), never deleted
  from §3.
* §3's diagram has the `**Legend:**` line right after it whenever the diagram carries at least
  one color class, and has no legend at all when it carries none.
* Every component in §4 has exactly one of `NEW`/`MODIFIED`/`REMOVED`/`UNCHANGED` — never
  `UNKNOWN`, never left blank.
* No component appears in §4, in a diagram, or in a data-contract producer/consumer field unless
  it passed the **COMPONENT INCLUSION RULE**.
* No component or contract name appears anywhere in the document that is not literally present in
  `TRANSCRIPT` or `CLARIFICATIONS`.
* Every ODCS schema field in §6 is either named by `DOCUMENTED_CONTENT` or structurally necessary
  — never merely plausible.
* No `<!-- -->` comment, `TBD`, `TODO`, empty table cell, bracketed instruction, or leftover
  template example name remains anywhere in the output.
* Every subsection that had nothing to say uses the one-sentence note prescribed for it, not a
  placeholder.
* The Decision section only lists actions traceable to §4/§5.

---

# OUTPUT FORMAT

Return exactly one Markdown document: the ADR itself, starting with its `# ADR — <title>` heading.
No commentary before or after it, no explanation of what you did, no code fence wrapping the whole
document (only the Mermaid/`text`/YAML blocks called for inside specific sections are fenced).
